WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.970
Part 8 of nine: Respond to concerns and share progress.

2
00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:08.620
The standards connected with this part are 14, 15.

3
00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:13.590
Here is the work this part helps you prepare: Identify

4
00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.170
an accessible concern pathway and draft a candid progress update

5
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.400
In the curriculum's reference index, standard 14 is described as:

6
00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.060
Accessible ways to address conflicts, complaints and grievances.

7
00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:32.090
In the curriculum's reference index, standard 15 is described as:

8
00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.910
Communication of progress to people and communities.

9
00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:38.000
Make it possible to raise a concern.

10
00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:41.330
How does someone tell us the service isn't working, and

11
00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:42.650
what should happen to that concern?

12
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.080
An accessible service also needs a usable way to say

13
00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:51.000
that something is not working. Standard fourteen addresses culturally and

14
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.700
linguistically appropriate ways to identify, prevent and resolve conflicts, complaints

15
00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:01.280
or grievances. Standard fifteen addresses communication about progress in implementing

16
00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:02.780
and sustaining CLAS.

17
00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:07.170
For an individual learner, the practical starting point is knowing

18
00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:10.390
the appropriate route and explaining it clearly. You do not

19
00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:13.510
need to personally investigate every concern. You do need to

20
00:01:13.510 --> 00:01:16.070
avoid leaving someone without a next step simply because the

21
00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:17.850
issue belongs to another part of the organization.

22
00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.960
Receive the concern respectfully.

23
00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:25.330
Let's work through a concern about our notice without promising

24
00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:26.730
an outcome we can't control.

25
00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:30.940
Imagine someone says that they could not understand the service-planning

26
00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.280
notice and missed the opportunity to ask for assistance. An

27
00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:37.480
unhelpful response would focus only on proving that the notice

28
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:41.120
was sent. A more useful response acknowledges the concern, clarifies

29
00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.100
what the person needs now, and follows the applicable process.

30
00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:48.150
Do not promise a particular outcome, a deadline you cannot

31
00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:52.790
control, or confidentiality beyond what the process permits. Explain what

32
00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:55.150
you can do, what will happen next and who is

33
00:01:55.150 --> 00:01:58.890
responsible. If there are immediate safety issues, legal deadlines or

34
00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:02.270
urgent service needs, use the relevant current procedure rather than

35
00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:04.090
waiting for a general improvement review.

36
00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.000
A concern may involve a misunderstanding, a service failure, discrimination,

37
00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:12.780
an accessibility issue or another matter requiring a specific route.

38
00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.240
Do not relabel it simply to make it fit the

39
00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.480
process most familiar to you. Obtain the appropriate guidance and

40
00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.900
preserve the person's opportunity to use available channels.

41
00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:23.430
Check the route itself.

42
00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:27.780
The concern route itself can be hard to use. What

43
00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:28.480
should we examine?

44
00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:32.750
Ask whether someone can find the concern process, understand it

45
00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:35.710
and use it in a suitable language and format. A

46
00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:38.850
complaint form that is available only through an inaccessible website

47
00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.610
can prevent the organization from hearing the very problems it

48
00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:42.230
needs to address.

49
00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:45.740
Consider whether the person is told how receipt will be

50
00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:48.680
acknowledged, what information is needed, and how to ask about

51
00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.640
progress. Use approved records and channels. Do not place complaint

52
00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.900
details or case information in this course's reflection notes.

53
00:02:56.510 --> 00:03:01.790
Staff should also know how recurring concerns inform improvement. Resolving

54
00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:04.690
one issue may be necessary without addressing the condition that

55
00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:08.410
caused it. If several people encounter the same unclear instruction,

56
00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:11.810
the organization may need to review the instruction as well

57
00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:13.150
as respond to each person.

58
00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:15.880
Communicate what has changed.

59
00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:19.950
When we share progress, how do we distinguish what we

60
00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:21.430
did from what actually changed?

61
00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:27.720
Standard fifteen asks organizations to communicate progress. Useful communication is

62
00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:32.340
understandable, accessible and honest about limitations. It explains what was

63
00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.540
done, what was learned and what will happen next.

64
00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:38.970
For our notice, a progress update might explain that the

65
00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.790
wording and assistance information were revised after review, describe how

66
00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:45.950
usability is being checked, and identify the next review point.

67
00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:49.150
It should not claim that all communication barriers have been

68
00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:51.130
eliminated because one document changed.

69
00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:55.620
Separate an action from a result. Updating a notice is

70
00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.600
an action. Evidence that people can use it more effectively

71
00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:02.340
is a result that requires checking. Keeping that distinction visible

72
00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:03.920
helps avoid overstating success.

73
00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:10.770
Standard fifteen reaches beyond internal updates: organizations communicate progress to

74
00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:15.010
stakeholders, the people they serve and the public. Appropriate authority,

75
00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:18.790
accessible communication and protection of private information still matter.

76
00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:22.300
Protect privacy and preserve trust.

77
00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:25.750
What needs to stay private, and how should we talk

78
00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:27.130
about incomplete evidence?

79
00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:32.140
Share only information approved for the audience and purpose. Avoid

80
00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:34.680
details that could identify a person who raised a concern.

81
00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:38.620
Follow the appropriate review process for public communication or organizational

82
00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:41.620
statements. This lesson does not authorize a learner to publish

83
00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:42.520
on behalf of the division.

84
00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:46.310
If the evidence is incomplete, say what is known and

85
00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:49.310
what remains uncertain. If a change did not help as

86
00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:52.570
expected, explain the next step rather than hiding the result.

87
00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:55.810
Clear limits are more useful than a broad assurance that

88
00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:56.870
everything is resolved.

89
00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:58.820
Practice the response.

90
00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:03.130
Let's practice a response and prepare a candid progress statement.

91
00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:06.960
In the activity, select a response to an access concern.

92
00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:10.280
Look for acknowledgment, a usable next step and an honest

93
00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:13.960
description of responsibility. Avoid an answer that dismisses the person,

94
00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.460
makes an unsupported promise or sends them elsewhere without explaining

95
00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:17.820
the route.

96
00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:21.830
Then draft a short progress statement for your own work

97
00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:25.790
example. Include the issue being addressed, the action taken or

98
00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:28.530
proposed, how its effect will be checked and the next

99
00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:31.710
review point. Keep the statement general and suitable for a

100
00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:32.410
learning exercise.

101
00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:34.980
Prepare to bring the work together.

102
00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.450
What do we now have ready for the final improvement

103
00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:38.810
plan?

104
00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.440
You now have the main elements of a practical improvement:

105
00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:46.260
an understanding of the person's task, a barrier to investigate,

106
00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:49.360
an appropriate response, the roles needed to carry it out,

107
00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:52.860
input from people affected, evidence and a way to respond

108
00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:53.860
and report back.

109
00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:57.950
The final lesson will help you assemble those elements into

110
00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:00.870
one usable plan. It is not a test of memorizing

111
00:06:00.870 --> 00:06:03.710
all fifteen numbers. It is a chance to show how

112
00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:06.970
the standards support a thoughtful decision and a concrete next

113
00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:07.630
step in your work.

114
00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:12.680
Let's work through the companion example: Describe progress without overstating

115
00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:12.840
it

116
00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:16.890
Here is the situation. A draft update says, 'The new

117
00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:20.650
notice has solved communication barriers.' Only the revision and distribution

118
00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:21.530
have been confirmed.

119
00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:25.140
What response would move this example forward responsibly?

120
00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:29.090
A more accurate update says that the notice and assistance

121
00:06:29.090 --> 00:06:33.330
information were revised, explains what review informed the change, and

122
00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:35.990
describes how usability will be checked at the next review

123
00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:36.290
point.

124
00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:41.220
And here is why that response fits. Clear communication distinguishes

125
00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:45.200
completed actions from results still being evaluated, while protecting personal

126
00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:45.700
information.

127
00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:50.130
Now make your own choice. Someone says the notice was

128
00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:53.350
difficult to understand and they could not request assistance. Which

129
00:06:53.350 --> 00:06:54.510
response is most useful?

130
00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:59.040
Option 1. Explain that the notice was sent correctly and

131
00:06:59.040 --> 00:06:59.720
close the matter.

132
00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:04.370
Option 2. Promise that the concern will be resolved tomorrow

133
00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:06.090
and kept completely confidential.

134
00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:11.640
Option 3. Acknowledge the concern, clarify the immediate need and

135
00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:13.980
explain the appropriate next step and responsible route.

136
00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:16.990
Pause the player now if you want to choose and

137
00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:20.250
explain your reasoning. You can use your own notes or

138
00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:24.130
the companion activity. When you're ready, resume and we'll work

139
00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:25.270
through all three responses.

140
00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:28.240
How should we think about option 1?

141
00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:32.690
Sending information does not establish that it was usable. Acknowledge

142
00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:35.370
the concern and follow the appropriate response process.

143
00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.100
How should we think about option 2?

144
00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:42.910
Do not promise an outcome, timing or confidentiality beyond your

145
00:07:42.910 --> 00:07:44.430
authority and the process's limits.

146
00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.360
How should we think about option 3?

147
00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:53.390
This supports a usable response without making unsupported promises. Urgent

148
00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:57.090
needs or specific complaints may require a particular procedure.

149
00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:01.160
Option 3 is the supported choice in this activity.

150
00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:04.710
You can choose another response and compare the reasoning.

151
00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:10.140
Identify the appropriate concern route. Draft a general progress statement:

152
00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:13.940
issue, action, evidence or limitation, and next review point.

153
00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:18.390
The working note from this part is: Concern route and

154
00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:19.250
progress note

155
00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:22.440
Pause the player if you want time to make that

156
00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:26.120
note. Keep it general, without names or private details. Thinking

157
00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:27.960
it through without writing is also fine.

158
00:08:28.510 --> 00:08:31.950
That completes part 8. You can return to any passage

159
00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:34.550
or take a break. The next part is Bring it

160
00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:35.630
together in your work.
